<Project>

  <!--
    Packed as build/JustDummies.DiagnosticCatalog.props — the name is not decorative. NuGet imports a
    package's build/<package id>.props and ignores a file called anything else, so a typo here is a
    catalogue that checks nobody and says nothing about it.

    WITHOUT THIS FILE THE CATALOGUE IS SILENT. The DCAT analyzers live in the DiagnosticCatalog
    foundation and, since its ADR-0038, reach a compiler only where a catalogue asks for them. A
    catalogue that ships no opt-in still delivers its constants, and delivers no DCAT0006 on the
    literals a consumer has not converted — a codebase nothing checks, indistinguishable from one with
    nothing to report.

    It is OUR file rather than the foundation's because NuGet imports build/ for a DIRECT reference and
    for nothing further out. That asymmetry is the only point in the mechanism where "somebody
    referenced this catalogue" is distinguishable from "somebody is downstream of something that did",
    and only this package sits at it. Which is what stops an application referencing a library that took
    this catalogue for its own suppressions from being analysed by a catalogue it never chose.

    The condition lets the consuming project win whichever order MSBuild reads the two in: false keeps
    the catalogue and declines the analysis, true asks for the checks from further out than a direct
    reference. Neither is a case this package handles.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnableDiagnosticCatalogAnalyzers Condition="'$(EnableDiagnosticCatalogAnalyzers)' == ''">true</EnableDiagnosticCatalogAnalyzers>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
